/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Wild Nature: survivors, shapeshifters and hidden worlds */
WB.L("tardigrades", "nature", "beginner", "Tardigrades: the toughest animals on Earth",
  "Smaller than a full stop, the eight-legged 'water bear' can survive being dried out for years, frozen solid and even exposed to open space.",
  [
    ["Meet the water bear", "Tardigrades are microscopic, eight-legged animals found almost everywhere, from backyard moss to the polar regions.",
      "Scrape moss off a garden wall, soak it in water and look through a microscope. You may see a plump creature lumbering along on eight stubby, clawed legs. This is a tardigrade. Most are well under a millimetre long, and the largest reach only about a millimetre and a half.\n\nA German pastor called one a 'little water bear' in 1773, and the name stuck. Tardigrada means 'slow steppers'. More than 1,300 species are known. They live in moss, lichen, soil, fresh water and ocean sediments, from mountaintops to the deep sea and the poles. Most feed by piercing plant cells, algae or other tiny animals with sharp mouthparts."],
    ["The tun: life on pause", "Tardigrades survive drying out by shrivelling into a dormant 'tun' in which their metabolism almost stops.",
      "A tardigrade's watery home can vanish in one hot afternoon. As the moss dries, many species pull in their head and legs, lose almost all their body water and shrivel into a little barrel called a tun. Their metabolism slows until it can barely be detected. This state is called cryptobiosis, meaning 'hidden life'.\n\nIn a tun, a tardigrade can wait out drought for years. When water returns, it rehydrates and starts walking again within minutes to hours. In one well-known case, Japanese researchers revived tardigrades from Antarctic moss that had been frozen for more than 30 years, and one went on to lay eggs."],
    ["Tough, but not invincible", "Tardigrades' extreme tolerance applies mainly to the dried tun state, so their real talent is switching life off and on.",
      "In laboratory experiments, dried tardigrades have survived chilling to within a degree or so of absolute zero and brief heating above the boiling point of water. They have endured pressures several times greater than the deepest ocean trench, and radiation doses hundreds of times higher than would kill a person.\n\nIt is easy to conclude they are indestructible, but they are not. Most of these feats apply only to dried tuns, and often only for short periods. An active, hydrated tardigrade is a fairly delicate animal, and long exposure to warm temperatures can kill it. Their real superpower is not toughness but the ability to switch life off and on."],
    ["Water bears in space", "Dried tardigrades were the first animals known to survive open space, but the Moon is probably not now home to any.",
      "In 2007 a European experiment launched dried tardigrades into low Earth orbit on an uncrewed Russian satellite. For about ten days they sat outside the spacecraft, exposed to vacuum and cosmic radiation. Back on Earth, many were rehydrated and recovered, and some went on to reproduce. Those that also faced unfiltered ultraviolet light from the Sun fared far worse, though a few survived. It was the first time any animal was known to survive open space.\n\nIn 2019 an Israeli lunar lander carrying dried tardigrades crashed on the Moon. Later laboratory tests, firing tardigrades from a gun, suggested the impact was probably too violent for them to survive."],
    ["Secrets and uses", "Special tardigrade proteins protect cells from drying and DNA damage, and may one day help store medicines without refrigeration.",
      "Drying normally destroys cells, because proteins unfold and membranes tear. Brine shrimp flood their cells with a protective sugar, trehalose, but many tardigrades make little of it. Instead, researchers have found proteins unique to tardigrades that seem to form a gel and then a glass-like solid as the cell dries, holding delicate molecules in place. Another protein, Dsup ('damage suppressor'), binds DNA and appears to shield it. Human cells given the Dsup gene suffered noticeably less X-ray damage in the lab.\n\nThis could matter: many vaccines need refrigeration, which is hard in remote places such as the outback. But the work is at an early stage and may take many years. You can meet tardigrades yourself by soaking moss in water overnight and scanning it under a simple microscope."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the name Tardigrada mean?", ["Moss dwellers","Slow steppers","Tiny survivors","Hidden life"], 1, "Tardigrada means 'slow steppers', a nod to their lumbering walk."],
    [1, "What happens to a tardigrade in the dried tun state?", ["Its metabolism slows to almost nothing","It lays eggs, then dies in the drought","It grows a hard shell around its body","It keeps feeding slowly on dust particles"], 0, "In cryptobiosis the animal loses nearly all its water and its metabolism almost stops, until water returns."],
    [2, "Which statement about tardigrade toughness is most accurate?", ["They are indestructible in every state","Active tardigrades survive boiling indefinitely","They tolerate extremes mainly when dried","They survive cold but never heat or radiation"], 2, "The extreme feats mostly apply to dried tuns, so their real talent is switching life off and on."],
    [3, "What did the 2007 orbit experiment show?", ["Dried tardigrades survived open space, and some bred","Tardigrades cannot survive any amount of radiation","Tardigrades survived on the Moon for many years","Hydrated tardigrades coped best with the vacuum"], 0, "Dried tardigrades were the first animals known to survive direct exposure to open space."],
    [4, "What does the protein Dsup appear to do?", ["Help them digest their prey","Make their cells glow in the dark","Protect their DNA from damage","Speed up how fast they reproduce"], 2, "Dsup binds to DNA and seems to protect it, which is why it interests radiation researchers."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Smaller than a full stop, it can switch life off for years. Meet the water bear."});

WB.L("mangroves", "nature", "beginner", "Mangroves: forests at the edge of the sea",
  "Muddy, smelly and buzzing with life, mangroves are trees that drink salt water, breathe through snorkels and quietly protect Australia's coasts.",
  [
    ["Trees in the sea", "Mangroves are a mix of salt-tolerant trees and shrubs that grow where tropical and warm coasts meet the tide.",
      "Walk a mangrove boardwalk at low tide and you hear snapping shrimp, popping air bubbles in the mud and crabs scuttling into holes. Twice a day, the sea floods in over the roots.\n\nMangroves are not one kind of plant but a mix of trees and shrubs from different families, each adapted to life where land meets salt water. They grow along sheltered tropical and warm coastlines worldwide. Australia has around 40 species and one of the largest areas of mangrove forest of any country, across the north and down both the east and west coasts. Around Darwin, Cairns and the Gulf of Carpentaria, they form vast, dense forests."],
    ["Salt, snorkels and stilts", "Mangroves cope with salt by filtering or excreting it, and breathe and stand firm in airless mud using specialised roots.",
      "Salt water kills most plants by drawing water out of their cells. Many mangroves filter salt out at the roots. Others take it in and then expel it: the grey mangrove, Australia's most widespread species, pushes salt out through glands in its leaves, and you may taste crystals on a leaf's underside. Some store salt in old leaves or bark and shed them. This costs energy, so growth is often slower in very salty places.\n\nMangrove mud has almost no oxygen. Grey mangroves send up pencil-like roots called pneumatophores that act as snorkels, taking in air through tiny pores at low tide. Red mangroves stand on arching prop roots. These tangles also anchor the trees and slow the water, so sediment settles and builds the shoreline."],
    ["Babies born ready", "Many mangroves germinate on the parent tree, then drop or float away as seedlings that can drift for weeks or months.",
      "Most plants drop seeds and wait for them to sprout. Many mangroves do something closer to giving birth. The seed germinates while still on the parent tree, growing into a seedling called a propagule. In some species these look like long green cigars or pens dangling from the branches.\n\nWhen ready, the propagule drops. If the tide is out, it may lodge in the mud and start growing straight away. If the tide is in, it floats off and can drift for weeks or even months before washing up somewhere suitable. This head start helps the young plant establish where waves and tides would sweep away an ordinary seed. It is also how mangroves have spread along coastlines and between islands across the tropics."],
    ["Nurseries and guardians", "Mangroves shelter young fish, protect shorelines from waves and lock away large amounts of carbon in their muddy soils.",
      "Calm water and tangled roots make an ideal nursery. Young fish and prawns hide from predators while feeding on fallen, decaying mangrove leaves. Barramundi, mud crabs, mangrove jack and banana prawns all depend on mangroves for part of their lives. Birds use them too. The rotten egg smell is not a sign of trouble: it comes from bacteria breaking down organic matter in oxygen-poor mud, part of the recycling that makes the system so productive.\n\nMangroves also act as a natural sea wall, absorbing wave and storm surge energy. Their waterlogged mud buries dead material, building deep, carbon-rich soil. Per hectare, these 'blue carbon' ecosystems can store several times more carbon than many land forests. Clearing them can release it."],
    ["Under pressure", "Clearing, development and climate extremes threaten mangroves, which are protected by law in Australia.",
      "Worldwide, large areas of mangroves have been cleared for shrimp farms, ports, housing and farmland. Australia's are in better shape than many, but not immune. In late 2015 and early 2016, mangroves along roughly 1,000 kilometres of the Gulf of Carpentaria died back over a few months, affecting thousands of hectares.\n\nScientists linked the event to drought, high temperatures and a temporary drop in sea level during a strong El Niño. Some areas have since begun to recover. Mangroves are protected by law in Australian states and territories. In Queensland, for example, they are protected marine plants, and damaging them without approval is an offence, so check local rules before trimming any. The simplest way to appreciate them is from a boardwalk."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly how many mangrove species does Australia have?", ["About 5","About 40","About 400","About 4,000"], 1, "Australia has around 40 mangrove species across its northern, eastern and western coasts."],
    [1, "What are pneumatophores?", ["Seeds that float away on the tide","Leaves that store excess salt","Snorkel-like roots that take in air","Roots that store water underground"], 2, "These pencil-like roots poke out of the mud and pass air to the buried roots."],
    [2, "What is a mangrove propagule?", ["A seedling that grows on the parent tree","A salt gland on the underside of a leaf","A root that props up the trunk in mud","A fruit that crabs bury in the mud"], 0, "Many mangroves germinate while still attached, then drop or float away as ready-made seedlings."],
    [3, "Why can the rotten egg smell of a mangrove forest be a good sign?", ["It shows bacteria recycling matter in the mud","It means the trees are slowly dying of disease","It keeps predators away from young fish","It comes from salt crystals on the leaves"], 0, "The smell comes from bacterial breakdown in the mud, part of what makes the ecosystem so productive."],
    [4, "What was linked to the 2015 to 2016 mangrove dieback in the Gulf of Carpentaria?", ["A major cyclone and a large oil spill","Drought, heat and a drop in sea level","Grazing by introduced feral goats","A fungal disease carried by birds"], 1, "Scientists linked the dieback to drought, high temperatures and a sea level drop during a strong El Niño."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"These trees drink seawater and breathe through snorkels. Here's how."});

WB.L("carnivorous-plants", "nature", "beginner", "Plants that eat animals",
  "Snapping traps, sticky tentacles and slippery pitchers: some plants have turned the tables on the animal world, and Australia is a hotspot for them.",
  [
    ["Why eat meat?", "Carnivorous plants eat animals mainly to gain nitrogen and phosphorus that their poor soils lack.",
      "Picture a peat bog: soggy, acidic and sunlit, with soil that holds almost no nitrogen or phosphorus. Most plants struggle there. Carnivorous plants thrive, because they trap and digest insects and other small creatures, absorbing the minerals the soil lacks.\n\nThey still make their own food from sunlight, like any green plant. Prey is more of a fertiliser supplement than a main meal. There are several hundred species, and the habit has evolved independently several times in unrelated plant groups, a sign of how useful it is in poor soils. Each group came up with its own kind of trap, from snapping jaws to sticky glue to water-filled pits."],
    ["Flytraps and pitchers", "A Venus flytrap counts touches before snapping shut, while pitcher plants rely on slippery, fluid-filled cups.",
      "The Venus flytrap grows wild only in a small area of North and South Carolina. Its trap is a pair of hinged lobes with a few trigger hairs. One touch does nothing. A second touch within about 20 seconds snaps it shut in a fraction of a second. This counting stops the plant wasting energy on raindrops. A struggling insect keeps brushing the hairs, which seals the trap and releases digestive juices. Digestion takes several days, and each trap can open and close only a limited number of times.\n\nPitcher plants use a passive trap: a deep cup of digestive fluid with a slippery rim. Insects tumble in and cannot climb out. Australia has native Nepenthes pitcher plants in far north Queensland."],
    ["Sundews and bladderworts", "Sundews trap prey with sticky droplets and Australia is a world centre of sundew diversity, while bladderworts suck in prey in a split second.",
      "Sundews look covered in morning dew, but each tentacle's droplet is sticky glue. A landing insect is held fast, and in many species the tentacles bend inward to press it against the leaf, where enzymes digest it. Australia is one of the world's great centres of sundew diversity, especially the south-west of Western Australia. Some are tiny pygmy sundews no bigger than a coin, and many survive dry summers as underground tubers.\n\nBladderworts, which live in water or wet soil, pump water out of tiny bladders to create low pressure. When a water flea brushes a trigger hair, a trapdoor opens and the bladder sucks it in within a tiny fraction of a second, one of the fastest movements known in plants."],
    ["The Albany pitcher plant", "The Albany pitcher plant grows wild only in a small region of south-west Western Australia and is the only species in its family.",
      "Australia has a true original: the Albany pitcher plant. It grows wild only in a small coastal region of south-west Western Australia. It is so distinctive that it is the only species in its plant family.\n\nIts small, hairy pitchers sit at ground level, catching mostly ants and other crawling insects.\n\nPlants like this are specialists of nutrient-poor wetlands and heaths, so they are vulnerable when those places change. Draining swamps, clearing land, adding fertiliser and suppressing the natural fires that keep competing plants in check can all push them out. Their striking looks also attract collectors, and poaching is a serious problem for the Venus flytrap and some Nepenthes species. Never dig carnivorous plants up from bushland."],
    ["Growing them at home", "Carnivorous plants need rainwater, poor soil and bright light, and should never be fed meat or fertiliser.",
      "Carnivorous plants break most gardening rules. Ordinary potting mix and fertiliser can burn their roots. Most are grown in peat moss or a substitute mixed with sand or perlite. Tap water often contains enough dissolved minerals to harm them over time, so use rainwater or distilled water, and many types like to sit in a shallow tray of it. Most need plenty of bright light.\n\nResist feeding them pieces of meat, which can rot in the trap, and avoid triggering flytraps for fun, since every closure uses energy. Outdoors, they usually catch all the insects they need. Buy plants from reputable nurseries that propagate their own stock, not plants that may have been taken from the wild."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do carnivorous plants catch animals?", ["They cannot make food from sunlight","To get nutrients that poor soils lack","To protect their flowers from pests","To collect water during long droughts"], 1, "Prey supplies nitrogen and phosphorus, a bit like fertiliser, while sunlight still provides their food."],
    [1, "What makes a Venus flytrap snap shut?", ["Any single touch anywhere on the leaf","Bright sunlight warming the open trap","Two touches to its hairs within seconds","Warm air from an animal's breath"], 2, "Counting touches stops the plant wasting energy on raindrops and debris."],
    [2, "Which part of Australia is a major centre of sundew diversity?", ["Tasmania's wet west coast","The treeless Nullarbor Plain","Central Australia's red desert","South-west Western Australia"], 3, "South-west Western Australia has an exceptional variety of sundews."],
    [3, "What is special about the Albany pitcher plant?", ["It is wild only in south-west Western Australia","It is the largest pitcher plant in the world","It grows wild across all of northern Australia","It catches mostly flying insects, such as moths"], 0, "It is found wild only in a small coastal region, and is the only species in its family."],
    [4, "What water is best for most carnivorous plants?", ["Tap water mixed with fertiliser","Rainwater or distilled water","Bore water high in minerals","Bottled mineral water from a spring"], 1, "Dissolved minerals in tap water can harm plants adapted to nutrient-poor conditions."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some plants eat animals, and Australia has some of the strangest."});

WB.L("camouflage", "nature", "beginner", "Masters of disguise: how animals hide",
  "A bird that becomes a broken branch, a fish that looks like seaweed, a cuttlefish that changes its skin in a blink: inside nature's art of hiding.",
  [
    ["A bird, a branch", "Camouflage is about defeating the eyes and brain of the observer, using behaviour and shape as well as colour.",
      "At dusk in an Australian park, you might walk right past a tawny frogmouth. By day this bird sits motionless on a branch, its mottled grey and brown feathers matching the bark. If it senses danger, it stretches, points its beak upward, narrows its eyes to slits and freezes, looking just like a broken stump.\n\nThe disguise works because of the whole package: colour, shape and stillness. That is the heart of camouflage. It is not just about being the right colour. It is about defeating the eyes and brain of whoever is looking, whether a hungry predator or prey to be ambushed. Over millions of years, animals have evolved many ways to do it."],
    ["Blending and breaking up", "Animals hide by matching their background, using countershading to cancel shadows, and breaking up their outline with bold patches.",
      "The simplest strategy is background matching, as with a grasshopper on dry grass. But light comes from above, so a body's top is bright and its belly shadowed, which makes it look solid. Many animals counter this with countershading: darker on top, paler underneath. It cancels the shadow, flattening the body. Many fish, sharks and dolphins use it. From above, dark backs blend with the deep water; from below, pale bellies blend with the bright surface.\n\nOur brains are good at spotting outlines, so another strategy is to destroy them. Disruptive colouration uses bold contrasting patches across the body's edges, so the eye sees separate shapes, not one animal. In the First World War, ships were painted in dazzle stripes to confuse submarine crews judging their speed and direction."],
    ["Pretending to be something else", "Masquerade works by looking like a boring or inedible object, such as a twig, leaf or seaweed, rather than by hiding.",
      "Some animals are perfectly visible but go unnoticed because they look like something boring or inedible. This is called masquerade. Stick insects look like twigs, and many sway as if in the wind. Leaf insects have fake veins and even nibbled edges. Some caterpillars and spiders look like bird droppings, which few predators want to eat.\n\nOne of the most beautiful examples lives in southern Australia's cool waters. The leafy seadragon, a relative of seahorses, is covered in leaf-like flaps of skin and drifts slowly through kelp and seagrass, looking like floating seaweed. It is the marine emblem of South Australia, and divers can stare at weed for a long time before realising it is looking back."],
    ["Mimicry and living screens", "Harmless animals can copy dangerous ones, and cuttlefish change colour in under a second using pigment sacs controlled by nerves.",
      "Dangerous animals often advertise with warning colours, such as the yellow and black of wasps. Predators learn to avoid them, which lets harmless species cheat. Hoverflies cannot sting but look like wasps: Batesian mimicry. When several dangerous species share a warning pattern, it is Müllerian mimicry. The mimic octopus of Southeast Asia can change its shape and movement to resemble a flatfish, a lionfish or a sea snake.\n\nCuttlefish skin holds thousands of elastic pigment sacs called chromatophores, each wrapped in muscles controlled directly by nerves. Stretching or squeezing them changes colour and pattern in well under a second, and bumps on the skin can match rough rock. Most appear colour-blind, yet match colours well. At Point Lowly, near Whyalla, small male giant cuttlefish have been seen posing as females to slip past larger rivals."],
    ["An endless arms race", "Camouflage evolves through natural selection, as predators and prey keep driving each other to improve.",
      "Camouflage is a product of natural selection. Individuals that are slightly harder to spot are more likely to survive and pass on their genes, while predators with sharper eyes catch more food. The result is an ongoing arms race.\n\nA classic example is the peppered moth in England. During the Industrial Revolution, soot darkened tree trunks, and a dark form of the moth, once rare, became common in industrial areas because birds found the pale moths easier to spot. After clean air laws cut pollution in the twentieth century, the pale form became common again.\n\nToday camouflage faces new challenges, as climate change shifts the timing of seasons. In some places, animals that turn white for winter are now sometimes stranded in white coats on brown, snowless ground."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does the tawny frogmouth hide when it senses danger?", ["It burrows into the ground nearby","It freezes and looks like a broken stump","It quickly changes its feather colour","It flies off into the dense grass"], 1, "Colour, shape and stillness together make it look like a broken stump."],
    [1, "What is countershading?", ["Being darker on top and paler below","Changing colour with the seasons","Copying the look of a dangerous animal","Having dark stripes across the eyes"], 0, "A pale belly cancels the shadow that would otherwise make a body look solid."],
    [2, "What is masquerade in camouflage?", ["Hiding inside a burrow or crevice","Looking like something a predator ignores","Copying the warning colours of wasps","Changing colour within a few seconds"], 1, "Masquerading animals stay visible but are mistaken for sticks, leaves, seaweed or droppings."],
    [3, "What lets cuttlefish change colour so quickly?", ["Hormones slowly released into the blood","Bacteria that live in their skin","Pigments absorbed from the food they eat","Pigment sacs worked by nerves and muscles"], 3, "Stretching or squeezing the sacs changes their colour and pattern in well under a second."],
    [4, "Why did dark peppered moths become common in industrial England?", ["Soot darkened trunks, so birds saw pale moths first","Dark moths breed faster in every condition","Birds always prefer to eat dark moths","Pollution changed the colour of individual moths"], 0, "Natural selection favoured whichever form was harder for predators to see."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A bird that becomes a broken branch. How animals vanish in plain sight."});

WB.L("fungi", "nature", "intermediate", "The hidden kingdom of fungi",
  "The mushroom you see is just the tip of the iceberg. Below ground, fungi feed forests, recycle the dead and sometimes grow into some of the largest living things on Earth.",
  [
    ["Not quite plants", "Fungi are a kingdom of their own that digest food outside their bodies and are more closely related to animals than to plants.",
      "After autumn rain, mushrooms seem to appear overnight. It is tempting to see them as odd little plants, but fungi are something else. They cannot make food from sunlight. Instead, they release digestive enzymes into their surroundings and absorb the nutrients that are broken down. Their cell walls are made of chitin, the tough material in insect shells, not plant cellulose. Genetic studies show fungi are more closely related to animals than to plants.\n\nThe kingdom includes yeasts, moulds, mushrooms, rusts and lichen partners. Around 150,000 species have been named, but the true total is estimated in the millions, and in Australia most fungi are thought to be undescribed."],
    ["The fungus underground", "A mushroom is only the fruiting body, and the real fungus is a network of fine threads called mycelium that can grow enormous.",
      "A mushroom is to a fungus roughly what an apple is to an apple tree: a temporary structure for spreading spores. The main body of most fungi is the mycelium, a web of microscopic threads called hyphae that spread through soil, wood or leaf litter. A teaspoon of healthy soil can hold a startling length of them.\n\nMycelium can grow enormous. In an Oregon forest, a single honey fungus spreads through the soil across nearly ten square kilometres, and is thought to be thousands of years old. It is often described as one of the largest living organisms on Earth. Above ground, you would notice only the occasional clump of honey-coloured mushrooms and some unhealthy trees."],
    ["Partners of plants", "Most land plants trade sugars for water and nutrients with mycorrhizal fungi, though claims about forests 'talking' go beyond the evidence.",
      "Most healthy plants have fungi wrapped around or inside their roots. These partnerships, mycorrhizas ('fungus root'), are formed by the large majority of land plants. The fungus's fine threads reach much further than roots, gathering water and nutrients such as phosphorus and nitrogen. In return, the plant gives the fungus sugars from photosynthesis. Some Australian orchids cannot even germinate without the right fungus.\n\nIn 1997 Suzanne Simard and colleagues suggested carbon could move between trees through shared fungal networks, and the 'wood wide web' idea took off. Networks linking roots are real, but how much they move between trees, and whether it helps, is still debated. A 2023 review argued the evidence for some popular claims, such as 'mother trees', was weaker than often suggested."],
    ["Nature's recyclers", "Fungi break down tough dead wood and leaves, returning nutrients to the soil that keep ecosystems running.",
      "Without fungi, forests would be buried in their own dead wood. Wood is hard to digest because it contains lignin, a complex material that stiffens plant cell walls. Few organisms can break lignin down efficiently, and white rot fungi are among the best.\n\nAs fungi, bacteria and small animals break down logs, leaves and dead animals, they release nitrogen, phosphorus and other nutrients back into the soil. A rotting log in Australian bushland may look like waste, but it is a busy recycling plant and a home for beetles, frogs, lizards and small mammals. That is one reason many land managers now leave fallen timber on the ground rather than tidying it away."],
    ["Friends, foes and safety", "Fungi give us food and medicines but can also be deadly, so never eat wild mushrooms you cannot identify with certainty.",
      "You probably ate fungi today. Baker's yeast releases carbon dioxide to make bread rise, and yeasts ferment beer and wine. Penicillium moulds make the blue veins in some cheeses, and koji mould makes soy sauce, miso and sake. In 1928 Alexander Fleming noticed a Penicillium mould killing bacteria on a dish, which led to penicillin. Cyclosporine, used to prevent organ rejection, also came from a fungus.\n\nBut the chytrid fungus has caused declines in hundreds of frog species worldwide, including in Australia. The death cap mushroom, introduced and found around Canberra and Melbourne, has caused deaths here, and cooking does not make it safe. This is general information: never eat wild mushrooms unless an expert has identified them, and if you suspect poisoning, call the Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which group are fungi more closely related to?", ["Plants","Algae","Animals","Bacteria"], 2, "Genetic studies show fungi are closer to animals than plants, and they feed by absorbing digested food."],
    [1, "What is a mushroom, compared with the whole fungus?", ["A temporary structure that spreads spores","The main feeding body, found underground","A poison produced by the fungus itself","The root system that anchors the fungus"], 0, "The mushroom is the fruiting body, while the mycelium underground is the main body of the fungus."],
    [2, "What is the fairest summary of the 'wood wide web' idea?", ["Studies have completely disproved the whole idea","Trees are proven to share messages and warnings","Fungal networks are real, but claims outrun the evidence","It only holds in tropical rainforests, not elsewhere"], 2, "Networks linking plant roots exist, but how much they matter between trees is still debated."],
    [3, "Why are fungi important in forests?", ["They break down dead wood and return nutrients","They stop fallen logs from decaying for years","They fix nitrogen from the air for the trees","They make food from sunlight for the forest"], 0, "White rot fungi and other decomposers keep nutrients cycling through ecosystems."],
    [4, "If you suspect mushroom poisoning in Australia, who should you call?", ["Your local council's rangers or parks team","A gardening club or plant nursery helpline","The Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26","No one, as cooking makes wild mushrooms safe"], 2, "Cooking does not make poisonous mushrooms safe, so seek expert help straight away."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The mushroom is only the fruit. What's underneath may be among the largest living things."});

WB.L("ants", "nature", "intermediate", "Ants: the superorganism",
  "There may be around 20 quadrillion ants on Earth. Individually simple, together they farm, build, wage war and solve problems no single ant could.",
  [
    ["A planet of ants", "Ants are astonishingly abundant, with about 20 quadrillion alive, weighing more than all wild birds and mammals combined.",
      "Sit on almost any patch of Australian ground for a few minutes and an ant will find you. In 2022, researchers combined hundreds of studies and estimated about 20 quadrillion ants on Earth, roughly two and a half million per person. Together, they estimated, ants weigh more than all wild birds and wild mammals combined.\n\nMore than 14,000 species have been named, and many more await description. Australia is especially rich, with well over a thousand known species. Ants live almost everywhere except the coldest places, from rainforest canopies to the hottest deserts, and they shape their environments by moving soil, spreading seeds and eating other insects."],
    ["One colony, one animal", "An ant colony works like a single organism, with queens that reproduce and female workers who do everything else, guided by chemical signals.",
      "A typical colony is a family. At its heart are one or more queens, whose job is laying eggs. Almost all the ants you see are workers, which are female and usually do not reproduce. Males appear only to mate, and die soon after. Workers divide the jobs of caring for young, building and defending the nest, and foraging. Biologists call the colony a superorganism, with workers a little like cells in a body.\n\nNo ant is in charge. They communicate mainly with pheromones. A forager that finds food lays a scent trail home. Shorter routes are reinforced faster, because round trips are quicker, so the colony settles on efficient paths without a plan. This emergent behaviour has inspired 'ant colony optimisation' in computing."],
    ["Farmers and herders", "Some ants farm fungus or herd sap-sucking insects for honeydew, practising agriculture long before humans did.",
      "Humans began farming roughly 12,000 years ago, but some ants have been doing it for tens of millions of years. Leafcutter ants of Central and South America do not eat the leaves they carry. They chew them into a pulp to feed a fungus in underground gardens, and the fungus becomes their food. Their nests can hold millions of ants.\n\nOther ants are herders. They protect aphids and scale insects from predators and collect honeydew, the sugary liquid these insects excrete. If you see ants running over a plant covered in scale insects, that is this partnership in action. Some ants even carry their insect 'livestock' to fresh plants to feed."],
    ["Australian ant marvels", "Honeypot ants store nectar in their swollen bodies, while green tree ants build nests by gluing leaves together with larval silk.",
      "In the arid centre, honeypot ant workers called repletes are fed so much nectar and honeydew that their abdomens swell into amber balls the size of a small grape. They hang from the ceilings of underground chambers as living food stores for lean times. Aboriginal people have long dug them up as a sweet food.\n\nIn the tropical north, green tree ants, also called weaver ants, build nests by pulling leaves together. Some workers hold the leaves in place while others carry larvae, whose silk glues the leaves together. Green tree ants are also a traditional bush food, and many people describe their taste as sharp and lemony."],
    ["Stings and invaders", "Bull ants and jack jumpers can trigger serious allergic reactions, and invasive ants can devastate wildlife and farms.",
      "Most ants are harmless, but bull ants and jack jumpers deserve respect. Their sting is painful. For a minority who are allergic, especially to jack jumper venom, it can cause life-threatening anaphylaxis. Jack jumper allergy is well known in Tasmania. This is general information, not medical advice: difficulty breathing, facial or throat swelling, or collapse need urgent help, so call 000. People with known allergies should follow their doctor's plan.\n\nInvasive ants are among the world's most damaging pests. Red imported fire ants were found in Brisbane in 2001 and have been the target of eradication ever since. On Christmas Island, yellow crazy ants formed supercolonies and killed millions of red crabs. If you suspect fire ants, report them to the national fire ant program or your state biosecurity agency."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to a 2022 estimate, how do ants compare with wild birds and mammals by weight?", ["Ants weigh far less than wild birds alone","Ants weigh about the same as wild mammals","Ants outweigh wild birds and mammals combined","Ants weigh about half as much as mammals"], 2, "Ants are so numerous that their combined weight exceeds that of all wild birds and mammals."],
    [1, "How do ant colonies tend to find efficient routes to food?", ["Shorter trails collect pheromone scent faster","The queen plans each route in advance","Workers vote on the best route at the nest","They follow the position of the sun only"], 0, "Round trips on shorter routes are quicker, so scent trails strengthen there first, with no leader needed."],
    [2, "Why do leafcutter ants carry leaves into their nests?", ["To eat them straight away","To feed a fungus they farm","To block the nest entrance","To feed herds of aphids"], 1, "They grow fungus in underground gardens, a form of farming that predates humans by millions of years."],
    [3, "What do honeypot ant repletes do?", ["Guard the nest from intruders","Sew leaves together with silk","Store nectar in swollen bodies","Farm fungus in underground gardens"], 2, "Repletes act as living food stores for the colony in lean times."],
    [4, "What should you do if you suspect red imported fire ants?", ["Dig up the nest to inspect it closely","Report it to your state biosecurity agency","Ignore them, as they are mostly harmless","Move the nest out to nearby bushland"], 1, "Reporting helps eradication efforts against a damaging invasive species."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Twenty quadrillion ants may outweigh every wild bird and mammal. How?"});

WB.L("eucalypts", "nature", "intermediate", "Eucalypts: trees built for fire",
  "Gum trees define the Australian landscape. Their oils, bark and hidden buds tell the story of a continent shaped by fire.",
  [
    ["Australia's signature tree", "Eucalypts are more than 800 mostly Australian species that dominate the country's forests and have adapted to both drought and fire.",
      "Step into the bush after rain and you may catch the clean scent of eucalyptus oil. Botanists group the eucalypts into three closely related genera: Eucalyptus, Corymbia (many bloodwoods and the spotted gums) and Angophora. Together they include more than 800 species, almost all native to Australia, with a few in New Guinea and nearby islands.\n\nThey dominate roughly three-quarters of Australia's native forests and grow in almost every environment, from snowy mountains to the dry inland. As the continent dried out and fire became more common, eucalypts became extraordinarily good at living with both drought and flame.\n\nForms vary widely: shrubby mallees grow as clumps of stems a few metres tall, while mountain ash is the world's tallest flowering plant, with one Tasmanian tree, Centurion, about 100 metres high."],
    ["Oil in the leaves", "Eucalyptus leaves hold aromatic oils that deter most animals and make eucalypt forests burn fiercely in extreme weather.",
      "Hold a eucalyptus leaf to the light and you may see tiny translucent dots. These are oil glands. The aromatic oils protect the leaves from many insects and animals. Only specialists, such as koalas, some possums and certain insects, cope with large amounts. People have long used eucalyptus oil in cleaning products and cold remedies.\n\nThe oils are also flammable, and on hot days some of these compounds evaporate from the leaves. Together with other natural chemicals released by the trees, they are thought to contribute to the blue haze that gives the Blue Mountains near Sydney their name. That volatility helps explain why eucalypt forests can burn so fiercely in extreme weather. Bark varies too: stringybarks are fibrous, ironbarks are hard and furrowed, and smooth-barked gums shed their outer layer each year."],
    ["Hidden buds and roots", "Many eucalypts survive fire by resprouting from buds protected under their bark or from a woody base called a lignotuber.",
      "A few months after a bushfire, blackened eucalypt trunks become covered in soft green and red shoots. Many eucalypts have epicormic buds, dormant buds hidden beneath thick bark along the trunk and branches. Fire may kill the leaves, but the insulated buds survive and sprout once the danger has passed, rebuilding the canopy far faster than a seedling could.\n\nMany species also have a lignotuber, a woody swelling at the base of the trunk, partly underground, packed with buds and food reserves. If the whole tree above ground is destroyed, it can resprout from this lump. Mallees are a classic example, regrowing from their lignotubers again and again after fire."],
    ["Trees that bet on seed", "Mountain ash is usually killed by intense fire and relies on seed, so fires returning too soon can destroy its forests.",
      "Not all eucalypts resprout. Mountain ash and its relative alpine ash are usually killed by intense fire. Instead, they bet on seed. Their gumnuts release seeds in huge numbers after the heat of a fire. The seeds fall onto nutrient-rich ash in full sunlight, and a dense, even-aged forest springs up.\n\nThis works well when big fires come at long intervals. The danger is fires returning too often. Young mountain ash take many years, often around two decades, to produce plenty of seed. If a young forest burns again before then, there may be no seed left, and the forest can be replaced by shrubs or other species."],
    ["People, fire and caution", "Aboriginal people have managed the land with fire for tens of thousands of years, and large gums can drop limbs without warning.",
      "Fire in Australia is not only a natural force. Aboriginal people have used it as a land management tool for tens of thousands of years. Cultural burning typically uses cooler, smaller fires at chosen times to create a patchwork of burnt and unburnt country, reducing fuel and encouraging fresh growth. Many practices were disrupted after colonisation, and more land managers now work with Aboriginal rangers to restore them. The Black Summer fires of 2019 and 2020 showed how hotter, drier conditions make fire seasons more dangerous.\n\nEucalypts are also planted worldwide, for timber and pulp, though some places criticise their water use and fire risk. At home, river red gums and other large eucalypts can shed big limbs suddenly, earning the nickname 'widowmaker', so avoid camping or parking under them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Roughly what share of Australia's native forests are dominated by eucalypts?", ["About one-tenth","About one-quarter","About three-quarters","Almost none"], 2, "Eucalypts dominate roughly three-quarters of Australia's native forests."],
    [1, "Why can eucalypt forests burn so fiercely in extreme weather?", ["Their flammable leaf oils vaporise in the heat","Their wood holds water that turns to steam","Their leaves are always dry and brittle","Their thin bark catches alight easily"], 0, "The same oils that deter animals are volatile and flammable."],
    [2, "What are epicormic buds?", ["Seeds stored inside woody gumnuts for years","Flowers that open only after heavy rain","Tiny oil glands scattered through the leaves","Dormant buds under bark that sprout after fire"], 3, "Insulated by bark, these buds survive fire and rebuild the canopy quickly."],
    [3, "Why can frequent fires threaten mountain ash forests?", ["Repeated fire makes their bark too thick","Young trees may burn before setting seed","Fire makes their seeds too heavy to spread","They resprout too slowly from lignotubers"], 1, "Mountain ash relies on seed, and young trees take around two decades to produce plenty."],
    [4, "Why do some large gums carry the nickname 'widowmaker'?", ["Their leaf oil is poisonous to people","Their roots can crack nearby houses","They can drop large branches suddenly","They attract dangerous snakes at night"], 2, "Big limbs can fall without warning, so avoid camping or parking beneath them."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Australia's gum trees are built to burn and regrow. Here's the trick."});

WB.L("animal-longevity", "nature", "advanced", "Animals that cheat ageing",
  "A shark born before Captain Cook sailed, a rodent that barely seems to age, a jellyfish that turns back its own clock: what nature's longest lives reveal about growing old.",
  [
    ["Ming the clam", "Some animals live for centuries, and their ages can be read from growth layers, raising the question of why ageing happens at all.",
      "In 2006, scientists dredging the seabed off Iceland pulled up an ordinary-looking clam. It was frozen on board as routine, so it was already dead when researchers counted the growth rings in its shell, much like tree rings, and realised it was extraordinary. The ocean quahog, nicknamed Ming, was about 507 years old, alive since around the start of the 1500s.\n\nMing is one of the oldest individual animals whose age has been reliably measured. Its story raises a big question: if some animals live for centuries, why do others, including us, age and die on a much shorter schedule?"],
    ["Why do we age?", "Evolutionary theory says ageing happens because natural selection weakens later in life, so safer lives should evolve slower ageing.",
      "Ageing is an evolutionary puzzle. Why would natural selection allow bodies to wear out? Biologists such as Peter Medawar and George Williams developed an influential answer. In the wild, most animals die young from predators, disease, accidents or starvation. Genes that cause problems late in life are rarely 'seen' by natural selection, because few individuals live long enough for them to matter. Some genes that help early, for example by boosting reproduction, might even cause harm later.\n\nA related idea, the 'disposable soma' theory, says bodies invest in reproduction rather than perfect long-term repair. It predicts that animals facing less outside danger should age more slowly. Many long-lived species fit that pattern."],
    ["Giants and mole-rats", "Greenland sharks and bowhead whales may live for centuries, and naked mole-rats show no rising risk of death with age.",
      "The Greenland shark lives in cold North Atlantic and Arctic waters. Sharks lack bony growth rings, so in 2016 researchers used radiocarbon dating on eye lenses, which form before birth and do not change. They estimated the largest shark was around 400 years old, with a wide margin of uncertainty, making it the longest-lived vertebrate known. Bowhead whales may live more than 200 years, and old harpoon tips from the 1800s have been found in whales caught in modern times.\n\nThe pink, almost hairless naked mole-rat of East Africa has lived over 30 years in captivity, where a mouse lives two or three. A study found its risk of dying did not seem to rise with age, unlike almost every other mammal. It also very rarely gets cancer."],
    ["Turning back the clock", "Some simple animals, like the 'immortal jellyfish' and hydra, show little or no sign of ageing, so ageing is not an unbreakable law.",
      "A tiny jellyfish, Turritopsis dohrnii, is nicknamed the immortal jellyfish. When injured, stressed or old, it can transform back into an earlier life stage, a polyp attached to the seabed, and grow into a new jellyfish. In principle, it could repeat this many times. In practice, individuals still die from predators and disease, so it is not truly immortal.\n\nThe freshwater hydra, a small relative of jellyfish, also appears to show little or no ageing. Stem cells constantly renew its body, and in laboratory studies hydra did not show the usual rise in death rate over time. These animals suggest ageing is not an unbreakable law of biology."],
    ["Cancer and our own lives", "Big, long-lived animals resist cancer through extra defences, but there is no proven shortcut to a much longer human life.",
      "Cancer starts when a cell picks up harmful mutations and divides out of control. So animals with more cells, living longer, should get more cancer. Yet large animals do not seem to have higher rates than small ones, a puzzle called Peto's paradox. Elephants have around 20 copies of the TP53 gene, which helps trigger the death of damaged cells, while humans have one pair. Bowhead whales appear unusually good at repairing DNA.\n\nThese animals give researchers real leads. But they do not yet translate into treatments that extend human life, and many anti-ageing products lack good evidence. This is general information, not health advice. The well-tested basics, such as not smoking, staying active, sleeping well and eating well, remain best supported."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How was the age of Ming the clam worked out?", ["Counting growth rings in its shell","Radiocarbon dating of its eye lens","Measuring how heavy its shell was","Reading its age from its DNA length"], 0, "Shell growth layers record age, much as tree rings do."],
    [1, "According to evolutionary theory, which animals should evolve to age more slowly?", ["Those facing more danger from predators","Those facing less danger from outside","Those that start breeding the earliest","Those that have the smallest bodies"], 1, "If few individuals die young, late-life problems face stronger selection, so slower ageing evolves."],
    [2, "What is unusual about the naked mole-rat's risk of dying?", ["It drops sharply after the age of two","It doubles with every year of life","It is higher than any other rodent's","It does not seem to rise with age"], 3, "Unlike almost every other mammal, its risk of death appears not to climb with age."],
    [3, "Why is the 'immortal jellyfish' not truly immortal?", ["It can still die from predators and disease","It ages in the same way as other jellyfish","It is unable to return to the polyp stage","It lives for only a few days at most"], 0, "Reversing its life stage does not protect it from being eaten or sickened."],
    [4, "What is Peto's paradox?", ["Small animals always outlive much larger animals","Big animals get no more cancer despite more cells","Cold-water animals always age much faster","Jellyfish can reverse their whole life cycle"], 1, "Big animals seem to have evolved extra defences, such as more TP53 copies or better DNA repair."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A clam was alive before Shakespeare was born. What do long lives teach us?"});

